'The wind blew, and the clouds broke up;
And we stood on the high road again;
We had no other guide than that.'

And now, like some young eagle that has left its nest,

From out the sea and ocean to come forth I go ;

But I am bound for better lands than these may boast —

Haiku
by Robert Frost

A red, red rose
Is very easy to grow—
All you need is soil and water;

The hardest part is
To keep it from dying before
Its beauty can be seen at all.* Write an acrostic poem on the word “summer.” Use each letter as the first letter for one line about summer.
Summer
Sunny days that are hot;
Evening breeze so cool;
Mosquitos bite me all day long;
Unpleasant to have them here!
Rainy days with thunder clouds;
Many different

The night is dark and deep;

And the world is all asleep ;
But, oh ! with what delight
I see thy dear sweet face bright !

To my love I'll cling
With my arms around her neck ;
She will lay her head on mine
In that quiet home at eve.

And the years shall roll, and the suns
Shall rise on worlds that are not ours;

* And the stars shall fall like rose-leaves
  From a tree of living gold,

Till each star be broken into dust,
Or its light grow dim with age or rust ;

The poem written by me is :

Lo ! there the Magnet attracts
What's drawn before and still attracts;
To this sublime Attraction we aspire;

The best union, which it inspires;
Uniting past and present from afar,
Our world united with what is here.

And so she walked away from him, for he had turned to her no more than an image on a wall ;

but when the sun was set that night , and all the world lay quiet , and still as death, —

She saw his face look up at her through the trees: “ And now we’re not speaking”

And why, oh! wherefore art thou gone?

The answer is, to drink wine, my love;

For all thy charms have lost their power,

Thou dost not care for them alone.

Wine takes away thy thoughts from thee,

Which makes me think we cannot meet;
